# Functional Hazard Assessment

## Purpose

This document provides a preliminary Functional Hazard Assessment (FHA)-style analysis for the compact avionics controller architecture evaluation.

The purpose is to identify controller-level functions, potential functional failure conditions, operational effects, and recommended mitigations associated with integrated avionics and independent navigator workflows.

> **Note:** This document is an independent systems engineering exercise conducted within a flight simulation environment. It is not a certification-level Functional Hazard Assessment, System Safety Assessment, or aircraft-level safety analysis.



## FHA Scope

This assessment focuses on the controller interaction layer between the pilot and simulated avionics systems.

The scope includes:

- FMS1 and FMS2 control behavior
- Integrated avionics workflows
- Independent navigator workflows
- Shift mode behavior
- Pan/zoom mode behavior
- Cursor mode awareness
- CDI/source control behavior
- Controller annunciation and mode awareness

The scope does not include:

- Aircraft flight control systems
- Certified aircraft avionics hardware
- Actual navigation system integrity
- Aircraft-level hazard classification
- Probability assessment
- Fault tree analysis
- Failure modes and effects analysis



## System Context

The controller is evaluated as a human-machine interface used to operate simulated avionics systems.

Two primary avionics architecture types are considered:

| Architecture | Example Systems | Control Model |
|-------------|-----------------|---------------|
| Integrated Avionics | Garmin G1000, G3000, G5000 | FMS1 and FMS2 operate as interfaces to a shared avionics system |
| Independent Navigators | Garmin GNS430/530, GTN650/750 | FMS1 and FMS2 represent separate navigator systems |



